# SAITELEMES v0 — telegrams between running agents

Status: v0, implemented (T-109, SRC-097). Decision: D-058. Builds on the S2 seam
bridge (`spec/04-SAIPEN-SEAM.md` "S2 as built", D-057).

## 1. Why

An agent's audit turns up a fact that belongs to another running agent: a SAIPEN
defect found while working in SAIMAIL, for example. Before v0 that fact reached the
other agent only through a human relay or a cold inbox, so the other agent kept
working without it. T-108 measured both failures. The protocolist learned of three
defects only after the operator asked for a telegram. The host's cross-session
message then waited for approval in the recipient session and expired.

A SAITELEME is the SAIMAIL answer. The acting seat tells one registered running agent
one thing, in one call, at discovery time. Delivery goes into the recipient's own
Post Office and needs no human relay and no network.

## 2. What a telegram is

An **ordinary sealed message**. Nothing on the wire is new:

| Part | Value |
|---|---|
| sender | the workspace seat, which must equal the acting SAIPEN seat |
| `K` | one kind from the closed SENV2 set; default `DISCOVERY` |
| `TOPIC` | `--topic`, else the SAIPEN Work id (`STATE.task`), else `saitelemes` |
| body | exactly one of: the ordinary one-line claim wrapper (`EV:0`, `U1`), or the S2 citation of one LOG event (`KIND:O`, `EV` = sha256 of the exact line) |
| SUBJ | the Work id when it is one token, else `saitelemes` |

There is **no `TELEGRAM` kind**: SENV2 kinds are a closed wire set (spec/02 §3). There
is **no importance field**: attention stays receiver-owned (spec/07). Urgency is not
something a sender can assert.

## 3. Commands

```
saimail-local saipen telegram  --workspace WS --to ALIAS (--claim TEXT | --event E-###)
                               [--kind K] [--topic T] [--seat S] [--project-root P]
saimail-local saipen telegrams --workspace WS [--topic T] [--scan-budget N] [--cursor N]
```

`telegram` reads the acting seat and Work id through the S2 bridge. It seals and
delivers through the unchanged `send_message` path. A citation body is written to a
private temporary file for that one call and never inside the workspace layout.

`telegrams` is the **turn-entry read**: the P1 metadata query with `state=UNREAD`,
bounded by the declared scan budget, optionally filtered to one Work id. It never
decrypts, opens, acknowledges or promotes anything, and it needs no SAIPEN project.
Since T-117 it also holds no secret: it loads the public-key-only header view
(section 7), so it never reads a private key or the OS credential store.

## 4. Refusals

The current checkout's [Work Desk admission](27-SAIPEN-WORK-DESK-v0.md)
(T-110) also applies before `telegram`: project IDENTITY and minimum STATE
facts are required; absent participation yields `SAIPEN_ADMISSION_REQUIRED`.
The standalone header-only `telegrams` query remains available without a
project. Use `brief` for an admitted query with current work context.

| Code | When |
|---|---|
| `SAIPEN_SEAT_MISMATCH` | the acting seat (`--seat` > `SAIPEN_AGENT` > `STATE.agent`) is not the workspace seat. A telegram never goes out under another agent's name, the defect T-108 found in SAIPEN itself. |
| `BAD_INPUT` | no body or two bodies; a kind outside the closed set (including `TELEGRAM`) |
| every S2 and workspace code | unchanged (`SAIPEN_EVENT_UNKNOWN`, `RECIPIENT_UNKNOWN`, ...) |

## 5. Invariants

- **I1.** A telegram is data. Command-looking claim text is delivered and opened as
  text; the turn-entry read carries headers only.
- **No authority by arrival.** A telegram creates no Work, moves no claim, writes
  nothing under SAIPEN memory and skips no WAIT.
- **Receiver-owned acceptance.** The recipient must already be registered (`recipient
  add`) and must accept the sender key. There is no discovery registry of agents.

## 6. What v0 does not do

It does not make sending **automatic inside SAIPEN**. The trigger ("a finding owned
by another live agent emits a telegram") and a SAIPEN turn-entry call to
`telegrams` belong to the SAIPEN protocol. They are filed with the protocolist:
`_SAIPEN/future_gate/FUTURE GATE — SAITELEMES AUTOMATIC AGENT TELEGRAMS_20260922.md`.
v0 makes the SAIMAIL side a single call on each end, so that hook stays small. There
is no daemon, no push notification, no network transport and no flood control beyond
the receiver's scan budget.

Update (T-117): SAIPEN has since built the turn-entry call (SAIPEN T-1497). The
automatic send trigger is still unauthorized on the SAIPEN side.

## 7. The SAIPEN turn-entry consumer (T-117, D-059)

SAIPEN T-1497 (`_SAIPEN/tools/saipen_engine/telegrams.py`) runs, at `continue` and
`status`, only when the per-machine carrier `SAIMAIL_WORKSPACE` is set and
`saimail-local` resolves on PATH:

```
saimail-local --json saipen telegrams --workspace $SAIMAIL_WORKSPACE --scan-budget 200
```

It reports counts, never sender text: `unread` from `match_count`, `on_current_work`
from `items[].topic` equal to the SAIPEN Work id, and `complete` when `exhausted` is
false. Any non-zero exit or non-object answer becomes its `ERROR` state.

**Consumed fields are a contract.** `LOCAL_WORKSPACE_COMMAND_1` keeps, for this
command: exit code 0 with `ok: true`, integer `match_count`, boolean `exhausted`,
`items[].topic`; a refusal exits 1 with `ok: false` and its code in `status` (there
is no `code` field). `tests/test_turn_entry_headers.py` pins these.

**No secret at turn entry.** The read runs unattended on every SAIPEN turn, so it
must not need a key. The CLI loads `saimail.workspace.load_workspace_headers`, a
`WorkspaceHeaders` view that carries the recipient public key and fingerprints only.
It runs every public check of the full load (marker fingerprints, identity field set
and marker agreement, os-store public projection and handles) and never builds a
private key or reads the credential store. A locked or prompting store therefore
still yields counts. `inbox`, `saipen enter` and `saipen brief` use the same view;
open, reopen, send, reply and `saipen telegram` keep the full fail-closed load. The
view proves the public identity, not possession of the private one: a raw identity
whose private key no longer matches the marker still lists headers, and every
key-using load refuses it.

| Refusal | When |
|---|---|
| `WORKSPACE_MISSING` | no marker at the supplied root |
| `INVALID_WORKSPACE` | any public marker/identity/peers inconsistency, exactly as the full load |
